<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<package xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/packaging/2015/06/nuspec.xsd">
  <metadata>
    <id>ailimits</id>
    <version>{{VERSION}}</version>
    <title>AI Limits</title>
    <authors>napxlexn</authors>
    <owners>napxlexn</owners>
    <projectUrl>https://napxlexn.github.io/ailimits/</projectUrl>
    <projectSourceUrl>https://github.com/napxlexn/ailimits</projectSourceUrl>
    <packageSourceUrl>https://github.com/napxlexn/ailimits/tree/master/installer/choco</packageSourceUrl>
    <bugTrackerUrl>https://github.com/napxlexn/ailimits/issues</bugTrackerUrl>
    <releaseNotes>https://github.com/napxlexn/ailimits/blob/master/CHANGELOG.md</releaseNotes>
    <licenseUrl>https://github.com/napxlexn/ailimits/blob/master/LICENSE</licenseUrl>
    <requireLicenseAcceptance>false</requireLicenseAcceptance>
    <!-- a CDN, not raw.githubusercontent.com: the moderator holds that as a
         Requirement, and pinning to the release tag is the form they ask for.
         pack.ps1 fills {{VERSION}} here too, so every release pins itself. -->
    <iconUrl>https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/napxlexn/ailimits@v{{VERSION}}/assets/icon.png</iconUrl>
    <tags>ai claude codex copilot antigravity grok cursor usage-limits widget overlay tray</tags>
    <summary>Desktop overlay showing AI provider usage limits</summary>
    <description>
A tiny native Windows 11 floating overlay that shows your AI provider usage
limits on the desktop, in the tray or on the taskbar: Claude, OpenAI Codex,
GitHub Copilot, Google Antigravity, xAI Grok, Cursor. It reads the tokens
the official CLI tools already store on the machine, read-only and only
after you allow each one (the first start asks, naming every file and
host), and never refreshes them. Cursor uses a sign-in of the app's own,
made once from the menu. It sends no telemetry and renders on the CPU, with
a measured idle load of 0.012% of one core (0.7.0).

The app updates itself silently from GitHub Releases by default (toggle in
the context menu): it checks each installer's SHA-256 and Ed25519 release
signature and installs only while you are away, so the installed version
may run ahead of the package version between pushes.
    </description>
  </metadata>
  <files>
    <file src="tools\**" target="tools" />
  </files>
</package>
